1. Difference Between Standard and Custom Objects
     1. Standard Objects
        ->  Pre-built by Salesforce
        ->   Available by default
        ->   Used for common business needs
        ->   Examples: Account, Contact, Lead, Opportunity
        ->   Cannot be deleted permanently
        ->   Already contain standard fields
        ->   Names do not end with __c
        ->   Created and maintained by Salesforce
        ->   Limited customization
   2.  Custom Objects
          ->  Created by users or developers
          ->  Need to be created manually
          ->  Used for specific business requirements
          ->  Examples: Student__c, Library__c, Hospital_Record__c
          ->  Can be deleted if not needed
          ->  Fields are created by the user
          ->  Names end with __c
          ->  Created and maintained by admin/developer
          ->  Highly customizable    
2.	What is a Record?
     ->  A Record is a single set of information stored in an object in Salesforce.
     ->  It contains data about one specific item or person.
     ->  A record is made up of different fields and their values.
     ->  Records are stored inside objects.
  Example:
    If Student is an object, then:
     -> Name: Ruksana
     -> Roll Number: 101
     -> Branch:IT
This complete information is called a Record.
Simple Understanding:
  -> Object = Table
  -> Field = Column
  -> Record = Single Row/Data inside the object

3.  What is a Lookup Relationship?
       ->   A Lookup Relationship is a loose connection between two objects.
       ->   Child object can exist independently
       ->   Used when objects are related but not fully dependent
     Example:
       Student → Course


4. What is a Master-Detail Relationship?
      ->  A Master-Detail Relationship is a strong parent-child relationship.
      ->  Child record depends on parent record
      ->  If parent is deleted, child records are also deleted
   Example:
       Course → Department


5.  Why are Relationships Important in Databases?
     -> Relationships help connect data between different objects.
Benefits:
    -> Avoid duplicate data
    -> Organize data properly
    -> Improve data management
    -> Represent real-world connections
Example:
    -> Students enroll in courses
    -> Faculty teaches courses
    -> Departments manage courses

